*%
*% ml1250.gpd - Samsung ML-1250, PCL5e
*%
*% A Unidrv v3 minidriver.  There is no compiled code in this driver at all:
*% Windows' in-box unidrv.dll does the rendering, halftoning, banding and
*% raster compression, and this file tells it which bytes the ML-1250 wants.
*%
*% Why PCL5e and not PCL6, when the printer advertises "Emulation: PCL6":
*% the ML-1250 has 4 MB of RAM and a year-2000 PCL-XL Protocol Class 2.0
*% interpreter.  Modern PCL6 drivers emit Class 3.0 streams and assume far
*% more memory, which is why they produce PCL XL error pages or nothing at
*% all.  PCL5e raster is the conservative subset this printer was sold with;
*% its own control panel has a PCL 5e menu and Samsung's Linux instructions
*% told users to pick "Samsung Printer PCL 5e Compatible".
*%
*% No device fonts are declared, so Unidrv renders every glyph into the
*% raster.  That is slower than downloading text, and deliberate: it means
*% Cyrillic, and any other script or font, prints exactly as it appears on
*% screen, with no symbol-set or font-substitution surprises.
*%
*% Geometry comes from the PCL 5 Technical Reference logical-page table.
*% Master units are 600/inch, matching <Esc>&u600D sent in CmdStartDoc, so
*% every coordinate below is in 1/600 inch.
*%
*% Keep every entry, and every brace, on a line of its own.  Unidrv's parser
*% silently drops an entry that shares a line with a brace, so a compact
*% "*Command: CmdSelect { *Order: ... *Cmd: ... }" compiles to nothing at
*% all.  tests/gpdcheck.py enforces this.
*%

*GPDSpecVersion: "1.0"
*GPDFileVersion: "1.0.0.0"
*GPDFileName: "ml1250.gpd"
*Include: "StdNames.gpd"
*CodePage: 1252
*ModelName: "Samsung ML-1250 PCL5e"
*MasterUnits: PAIR(600, 600)
*ResourceDLL: "unires.dll"
*PrinterType: PAGE
*MaxCopies: 99
*FontCartSlots: 0
*PrintRate: 12
*PrintRateUnit: PPM

*% ------------------------------------------------------------ capabilities

*% The printer cannot rotate anything itself, so Unidrv rotates the raster
*% for landscape.  That is one less thing the old firmware has to get right.
*RotateCoordinate?: FALSE
*RotateRaster?: FALSE
*RotateFont?: FALSE
*MemoryUsage: LIST(FONT, RASTER)
*EjectPageWithFF?: TRUE
*OutputDataFormat: H_BYTE
*OptimizeLeftBound?: TRUE
*RasterSendAllData?: FALSE

*% Blanks inside a scan line are NOT strippable here.  PCL is explicit that
*% once raster graphics mode is entered only Transfer Raster Data, Set
*% Compression Method and Raster Y Offset are accepted, and anything else
*% implicitly ends the image - so there is no way to jump over a gap in the
*% middle of a line.  Leading and trailing blanks are fine: those move the
*% left graphics margin before the image starts.
*StripBlanks: LIST(LEADING, TRAILING)

*% Same rule, stated the way Unidrv needs to hear it: no cursor move of any
*% kind is legal inside raster mode.  Without this Unidrv would happily emit
*% an <Esc>*p move between bands and silently terminate the image; with it,
*% Unidrv ends the raster, moves, and starts a new one, which is valid PCL.
*BadCursorMoveInGrxMode: LIST(X_PORTRAIT, X_LANDSCAPE, Y_PORTRAIT, Y_LANDSCAPE)

*CursorXAfterSendBlockData: AT_GRXDATA_ORIGIN
*CursorYAfterSendBlockData: AUTO_INCREMENT
*XMoveUnit: 600
*YMoveUnit: 600
*XMoveThreshold: 0
*YMoveThreshold: 0

*% ----------------------------------------------------------------- job

*% The job opens with the universal exit language command and @PJL JOB.
*% @PJL JOB is itself a reset, so every @PJL SET has to come after it - those
*% are emitted by the feature options below, at JOB_SETUP.5 and later.
*% @PJL ENTER LANGUAGE waits until DOC_SETUP so the whole PJL block stays
*% together and is not interrupted by PCL.
*%
*% A literal percent sign in a command string MUST be written %%.  In the
*% compiled GPD a single % introduces a parameter reference (%0001 = the
*% first parameter), and Unidrv does not escape it for you - not even when
*% it comes from a hex block.  A bare <25> in the UEL made Unidrv read
*% "%-123" as parameter number -123 and crash the spooler on the very first
*% command of every job.  Microsoft's own PJL.GPD writes "<1B>%%-12345X".
*Command: CmdStartJob
{
    *Order: JOB_SETUP.1
    *Cmd: "<1B>%%-12345X@PJL JOB<0D0A>"
}

*Command: CmdStartDoc
{
    *% Enter PCL, reset, then <Esc>&u600D to make cursor units 1/600 inch,
    *% which is what MasterUnits above promises.  The margins are set per
    *% page in CmdStartPage, because the page size command resets them.
    *Order: DOC_SETUP.1
    *Cmd: "@PJL ENTER LANGUAGE=PCL<0D0A><1B>E<1B>&u600D<1B>*r0F"
}

*Command: CmdStartPage
{
    *% Portrait (Unidrv rotates landscape itself), top margin zero and
    *% perforation skip off, so that cursor Y=0 is the top of the logical
    *% page.  This has to come after the page size and paper source commands
    *% in DOC_SETUP: PCL resets the top margin to 1/2 inch on a page size or
    *% orientation change.  Same order as ml1250.exe, which is known to print.
    *% Raster resolution goes here too, after the <Esc>E in CmdStartDoc.
    *Order: PAGE_SETUP.1
    *Cmd: "<1B>&l0O<1B>&l0E<1B>&l0L<1B>*t" %d{GraphicsXRes} "R"
}

*Command: CmdCopies
{
    *Order: DOC_SETUP.5
    *Cmd: "<1B>&l" %d[1,99]{NumOfCopies} "X"
}

*Command: CmdEndJob
{
    *% <Esc>E first so a printer that ignored the PJL still resets.  Nothing
    *% may follow the closing UEL.
    *Order: JOB_FINISH.1
    *Cmd: "<1B>E<1B>%%-12345X@PJL EOJ<0D0A><1B>%%-12345X"
}

*% -------------------------------------------------------------- cursor

*Command: CmdCR: "<0D>"
*Command: CmdLF: "<0A>"
*Command: CmdFF: "<0C>"
*Command: CmdXMoveAbsolute: "<1B>*p" %d{DestX} "X"
*Command: CmdYMoveAbsolute: "<1B>*p" %d{DestY} "Y"
*Command: CmdXMoveRelRight: "<1B>*p+" %d{DestXRel} "X"
*Command: CmdXMoveRelLeft:  "<1B>*p-" %d{DestXRel} "X"
*Command: CmdYMoveRelDown:  "<1B>*p+" %d{DestYRel} "Y"
*Command: CmdYMoveRelUp:    "<1B>*p-" %d{DestYRel} "Y"

*% -------------------------------------------------------------- raster

*% <Esc>*rB rather than <Esc>*rC: the C form also resets the compression
*% method to 0 and the left graphics margin, which would silently desync
*% Unidrv's idea of the current compression method between bands.
*Command: CmdBeginRaster: "<1B>*r1A"
*Command: CmdEndRaster: "<1B>*rB"
*Command: CmdSendBlockData: "<1B>*b" %d{NumOfDataBytes} "W"

*% Declaring both lets Unidrv compress every scan line both ways and keep
*% the smaller result.  TIFF 4.0 is PCL method 2, DRC is PCL method 3.
*Command: CmdEnableTIFF4: "<1B>*b2M"
*Command: CmdEnableDRC: "<1B>*b3M"
*Command: CmdDisableCompression: "<1B>*b0M"

*% ------------------------------------------------------------ features

*Feature: Orientation
{
    *rcNameID: =ORIENTATION_DISPLAY
    *DefaultOption: PORTRAIT
    *Option: PORTRAIT
    {
        *rcNameID: =PORTRAIT_DISPLAY
    }
    *Option: LANDSCAPE_CC270
    {
        *rcNameID: =LANDSCAPE_DISPLAY
    }
}

*Feature: Resolution
{
    *rcNameID: =RESOLUTION_DISPLAY
    *% The PJL resolution is what sizes the printer's page buffer; the PCL
    *% raster resolution (<Esc>*t#R, in CmdStartPage) only says how to scale
    *% the data into it.  Both are sent, as ml1250.exe does.
    *DefaultOption: Option600
    *Option: Option600
    {
        *Name: "600 x 600 dots per inch"
        *DPI: PAIR(600, 600)
        *TextDPI: PAIR(600, 600)
        *SpotDiameter: 100
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.5
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET RESOLUTION=600<0D0A>"
        }
    }
    *Option: Option300
    {
        *% The safety valve.  At 300 dpi the printer expands each source
        *% pixel to a 2x2 cell, so both the stream and the printer's own page
        *% buffer shrink fourfold.  Use it if a page fails with a memory error.
        *Name: "300 x 300 dots per inch"
        *DPI: PAIR(300, 300)
        *TextDPI: PAIR(300, 300)
        *SpotDiameter: 100
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.5
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET RESOLUTION=300<0D0A>"
        }
    }
}

*Feature: PaperSize
{
    *rcNameID: =PAPER_SIZE_DISPLAY
    *DefaultOption: A4

    *% PrintableArea is the PCL logical page width by the physical height
    *% less the 1/6 inch unprintable band top and bottom.  CursorOrigin is
    *% where PCL puts cursor (0,0): the logical page corner, which is inset
    *% horizontally but flush with the top edge of the sheet.

    *Option: A4
    {
        *rcNameID: =A4_DISPLAY
        *PrintableArea: PAIR(4676, 6814)
        *PrintableOrigin: PAIR(142, 100)
        *CursorOrigin: PAIR(142, 0)
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.10
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l26A"
        }
    }
    *Option: LETTER
    {
        *rcNameID: =LETTER_DISPLAY
        *PrintableArea: PAIR(4800, 6400)
        *PrintableOrigin: PAIR(150, 100)
        *CursorOrigin: PAIR(150, 0)
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.10
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l2A"
        }
    }
    *Option: LEGAL
    {
        *rcNameID: =LEGAL_DISPLAY
        *PrintableArea: PAIR(4800, 8200)
        *PrintableOrigin: PAIR(150, 100)
        *CursorOrigin: PAIR(150, 0)
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.10
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l3A"
        }
    }
    *Option: EXECUTIVE
    {
        *rcNameID: =EXECUTIVE_DISPLAY
        *PrintableArea: PAIR(4050, 6100)
        *PrintableOrigin: PAIR(150, 100)
        *CursorOrigin: PAIR(150, 0)
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.10
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l1A"
        }
    }
    *Option: A5
    {
        *rcNameID: =A5_DISPLAY
        *PrintableArea: PAIR(3212, 4760)
        *PrintableOrigin: PAIR(142, 100)
        *CursorOrigin: PAIR(142, 0)
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.10
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l25A"
        }
    }
    *Option: B5
    {
        *rcNameID: =B5_DISPLAY
        *PrintableArea: PAIR(4016, 5870)
        *PrintableOrigin: PAIR(142, 100)
        *CursorOrigin: PAIR(142, 0)
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.10
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l45A"
        }
    }
}

*Feature: InputBin
{
    *rcNameID: =PAPER_SOURCE_DISPLAY
    *DefaultOption: AUTO
    *Option: AUTO
    {
        *rcNameID: =AUTO_DISPLAY
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.30
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l7H"
        }
    }
    *Option: UPPER
    {
        *rcNameID: =UPPER_TRAY_DISPLAY
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.30
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l1H"
        }
    }
    *Option: MANUAL
    {
        *rcNameID: =MANUAL_FEED_DISPLAY
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: DOC_SETUP.30
            *Cmd: "<1B>&l2H"
        }
    }
}

*Feature: Halftone
{
    *rcNameID: =HALFTONING_DISPLAY
    *% A coarse clustered dither is the default on purpose.  It matters more
    *% than image quality here: clustered patterns produce long runs of equal
    *% bytes, which is what TIFF4 and delta-row compression need, and what the
    *% printer's own page compression needs even more.  Supercell and
    *% error-diffusion-like patterns produce high-entropy noise - HP documents
    *% error diffusion by name as the case a PCL5 printer's internal
    *% compression cannot handle - and on 4 MB that is the difference between
    *% a printed page and a print overrun.
    *DefaultOption: HT_PATSIZE_6x6_M
    *Option: HT_PATSIZE_6x6_M
    {
        *rcNameID: =HT_DITHER6X6_DISPLAY
    }
    *Option: HT_PATSIZE_8x8_M
    {
        *rcNameID: =HT_DITHER8X8_DISPLAY
    }
    *Option: HT_PATSIZE_SUPERCELL_M
    {
        *rcNameID: =HT_SUPERCELL_DISPLAY
    }
    *Option: HT_PATSIZE_AUTO
    {
        *rcNameID: =HT_AUTO_SELECT_DISPLAY
    }
}

*Feature: EconoMode
{
    *Name: "Toner Save"
    *FeatureType: DOC_PROPERTY
    *DefaultOption: OFF
    *Option: OFF
    {
        *rcNameID: =OFF_DISPLAY
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.10
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET ECONOMODE=OFF<0D0A>"
        }
    }
    *Option: ON
    {
        *rcNameID: =ON_DISPLAY
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.10
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET ECONOMODE=ON<0D0A>"
        }
    }
}

*% Samsung calls this SRT on the printer's own panel; over the wire it is
*% plain PJL RET.  The token set comes from Samsung's Remote Control Panel
*% binary: NORMAL, ENHANCE, GRAY, GRAY+.  "Printer default" sends nothing and
*% leaves whatever is configured on the panel alone.
*Feature: SRTMode
{
    *Name: "Resolution enhancement (SRT)"
    *FeatureType: DOC_PROPERTY
    *DefaultOption: PrinterDefault
    *Option: PrinterDefault
    {
        *Name: "Printer default"
    }
    *Option: Normal
    {
        *Name: "Normal"
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.20
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET RET=NORMAL<0D0A>"
        }
    }
    *Option: TextEnhance
    {
        *Name: "Text enhance"
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.20
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET RET=ENHANCE<0D0A>"
        }
    }
    *Option: ImageEnhance
    {
        *Name: "Image enhance"
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.20
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET RET=GRAY+<0D0A>"
        }
    }
}

*% Off by default and deliberately so.  Page protection asks the printer to
*% reserve a whole page buffer, and a stock 4 MB ML-1250 has nowhere to put
*% one - Samsung's own driver assumed at least 16 MB.  Turn it on only after
*% fitting more memory, and only if pages are failing with a print overrun.
*Feature: PageProtection
{
    *Name: "Page protection (needs extra printer memory)"
    *FeatureType: PRINTER_PROPERTY
    *DefaultOption: OFF
    *Option: OFF
    {
        *rcNameID: =OFF_DISPLAY
    }
    *Option: AUTO
    {
        *Name: "Auto"
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.30
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET PAGEPROTECT=AUTO<0D0A>"
        }
    }
    *Option: ON
    {
        *rcNameID: =ON_DISPLAY
        *Command: CmdSelect
        {
            *Order: JOB_SETUP.30
            *Cmd: "@PJL SET PAGEPROTECT=ON<0D0A>"
        }
    }
}
